  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`, index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has 33 fields, including `Cancelled`, `Carrier`, `Dest`, `Origin`, `FlightDelay` and `AvgTicketPrice`. Its time field is `timestamp`.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-bcbbabf3 Flights' (id `71655523-5814-4c66-8381-7a99af6f3639`) has two panels. One is a line chart of flight count over time. The other is a bar chart of flight count by `Carrier`.\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-bcbbabf3 Cancelled flights' (id `c8cf11f3-4769-40ea-b08b-a7f451efbcd2`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute and counts documents with `Cancelled: true` in a 5-minute window. It fires when the count is above 10. I created it as enabled, so no separate enable step was needed.\n4. **Edits and read-back:** I updated the carrier panel's title to 'Flights by carrier', and the update response shows the new title. Reading the rule back returned `enabled: true`.\n\nI didn't open the dashboard in Kibana's UI or check whether the rule has fired.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
